{
  "dom_id": "step-q142715",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $65%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*",
  "worked_solution": "Since $n=100$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=65%=0.65,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=100\\cdot 0.65=65>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=100\\cdot (1-0.65)=35>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14191704/assets/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.65\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.65\\pm 0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$",
